29	doc.push(P("The two probabilities differ by a factor of 40. The A1 note explains why it was lowered: \"Even 2% per missed defect would imply dozens of billion-dollar events annually nationwide — physically impossible. 0.05% keeps the implied nationwide rate roughly consistent with observed East Palestine-scale frequency.\""));
30	doc.push(P("D08's benefit figures, meanwhile, are reproducible if a prevented defect is valued at the contained-tier midpoint of $9.25M and nothing else: 0.65 × $9.25M ≈ $6.0M and 0.86 × $9.25M ≈ $8.0M, which is exactly D08's $6–8M pilot-year band at 0.7 defects missed. So D08 was built on the contained-tier value without the probability blend, and A1 applies the blend — a factor of roughly 9.25."));
31	doc.push(callout(
32	  "Why this one matters most",
33	  "These are the headline numbers in the document a CFO reads. D08 §5 states that A1 is the instrument of record and that \"this document must never be used to present one\" of these figures. The two disagree by nearly an order of magnitude at identical inputs. One of two things must happen: either D08 §5–§6 is restated from A1's output, or A1's evaluation assumptions are judged too conservative and the workbook is changed. Deciding that changes what the pilot pitch asks a customer to believe — which is why it is an owner decision and not an editorial one.",
34	  { fill: "FBEAEA", labelColor: RED },
35	));
