355:doc.push(P("Every warrant carries a SHA-256 hash over a canonical JSON serialisation of its advisory ID, asset, timestamp, full evidence array and both model and reference-population versions. That is what makes the record tamper-evident at the individual-warrant level. The disposition ledger, which is a different record, is now append-only, hash-chained and signed (Stage 7), and the residual limits of that claim are in Part VII."));
494:doc.push(P("The highest-value item in the handoff and untouched since cf2e6ba. assets/a2-d08-arithmetic-pass.py recomputes every figure in A2 and D08 from its stated source equation and exits non-zero on any mismatch. 78 of 78 computed figures agreed, so no arithmetic error exists in either document. Two claims did not reproduce — the two carried into Part VII as Findings 1 and 2 — and both were left open because fixing them means choosing which model governs, which is an owner decision rather than an editorial one."));
576:  "Sheet 1 (Fleet) takes axles, speed, site count and the customer's exposure, then derives a blended expected cost per missed defect. Sheet 2 (Detection) compares legacy and AxiomDynamics detection times and converts lead time into track miles of early warning. Sheet 3 (Benefit) computes prevented-failure cost minus false-alarm disruption. Sheet 4 (Licensing Cost) applies the two pricing axes. Sheet 5 (Summary) produces the three-year business case, ROI multiple and payback period. Every sourced figure is cited and every assumption is labelled in the cell beside it. Note: the committed workbook's cost basis does not match A2 — see the arithmetic finding in Part VII.");
792:doc.push(H1("Part VII — Open items and unresolved findings"));
861:doc.push(H1("Part VIII — How to resume"));

