doc.push(P("The decision paper then found a third basis that does reproduce them, and this is the correction worth carrying. On pilot cost plus three full years at list price with no conversion credit, the cost ratio the anchors demand — cost at 10,000 axles over cost at 500 = 9.756 — is a property of the pricing, not of the benefit model, so a single value of the catastrophic-escalation probability fits both endpoints. Against three years of benefit that value is p = 0.0714%, producing 2.00× and 4.06×; against four years of benefit it is 0.0412%, which also fits. Finding 1 was therefore a disclosure defect — an unstated frame, not an invention — but the parameter was not uniquely determined, because the benefit horizon was stated nowhere."));
doc.push(P("**How it closed.** The owner delegated the choice in chat-4 (\"you decide\") and A1 was chosen, expressed as Option D in `assets/a2-d08-model-decision-2026-11-09.md`. A2 §3.2 and D08 §3 now state **1.77× at 500 axles and 3.36× at 10,000**, with the cost basis written into the sentence rather than left to inference: A1 at its own defaults, three years of benefit against the four-year fee commitment at list price net of the §9 conversion credit. The text also states the fallback a reader needs — **1.65× and 3.34×** on a basis that additionally disallows the conversion credit — and warns against mixing the two in one table. The withdrawn pair is not reproducible on any basis in the repository, and that sentence is now in the arithmetic pass as a checked statement rather than a claim."));
